{"type":"data","nodes":[{"type":"data","data":[{"docsStarts":1},{"foundation":2,"assurance":3},"/foundation/docs/","/assurance/docs/getting-started/"],"uses":{}},null,{"type":"data","data":[{"docs":1,"source":44},{"nav":2,"slugs":43,"first":6},[3,7,11,15,19,23,27,31,35,39],{"name":4,"href":5,"slug":6},"Getting started","/assurance/docs/getting-started/","getting-started",{"name":8,"href":9,"slug":10},"Scenarios","/assurance/docs/scenarios/","scenarios",{"name":12,"href":13,"slug":14},"Business context","/assurance/docs/business-context/","business-context",{"name":16,"href":17,"slug":18},"Evidence","/assurance/docs/evidence/","evidence",{"name":20,"href":21,"slug":22},"The CLI","/assurance/docs/cli/","cli",{"name":24,"href":25,"slug":26},"Running in CI","/assurance/docs/ci/","ci",{"name":28,"href":29,"slug":30},"Adapters","/assurance/docs/adapters/","adapters",{"name":32,"href":33,"slug":34},"Analyzers","/assurance/docs/analyzers/","analyzers",{"name":36,"href":37,"slug":38},"Privacy","/assurance/docs/privacy/","privacy",{"name":40,"href":41,"slug":42},"Performance","/assurance/docs/performance/","performance",[6,10,14,18,22,26,30,34,38,42],{"repo":45,"version":46},"https://github.com/AHRConsulting/Ahr.Assurance","0.1.0"],"uses":{"params":["product"]}},{"type":"data","data":[{"slug":1,"page":2},"ci",{"slug":1,"title":3,"html":4,"headings":5,"source":16},"Running in CI","\u003Cp>Build the report after the tests:\u003C/p>\n\u003Cpre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003Ccode>\u003Cspan class=\"line\">\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-function)\">dotnet\u003C/span>\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-string)\"> test\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\n\u003Cspan class=\"line\">\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-function)\">dotnet\u003C/span>\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-string)\"> assurance\u003C/span>\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-string)\"> report\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/code>\u003C/pre>\n\u003Cp>Run the report step even when \u003Ccode>dotnet test\u003C/code> fails, so the report shows what failed. \u003Ccode>dotnet assurance report\u003C/code> exits with 2 when the report shows failures, and with 1 when it couldn't run: see \u003Ca href=\"/assurance/docs/cli/#exit-codes\">exit codes\u003C/a>.\u003C/p>\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-run-across-shards\">One run across shards\u003C/h2>\n\u003Cp>When the tests run in several jobs, give them one run ID, so every shard writes to the same run. Use the pipeline's own build number:\u003C/p>\n\u003Cpre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003Ccode>\u003Cspan class=\"line\">\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-keyword)\">export\u003C/span>\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-fg)\"> ASSURANCE_RUN_ID\u003C/span>\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-keyword)\">=\u003C/span>\u003Cspan style=\"color:var(--ahr-code-fg)\">build-1234\u003C/span>\u003C/span>\u003C/code>\u003C/pre>\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-report\">Share the report\u003C/h2>\n\u003Cp>Upload \u003Ccode>report.html\u003C/code> and the \u003Ccode>attachments\u003C/code> folder beside it as one artifact. The report works offline, so anyone can open it from the download.\u003C/p>\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trx-and-other-test-results\">TRX and other test results\u003C/h2>\n\u003Cp>Assurance doesn't replace or interfere with TRX or other test result files, and scenarios run side by side with your other tests. Test results record outcomes for CI tools; Assurance records the scenarios behind them, for people to read.\u003C/p>\n",[6,10,13],{"id":7,"text":8,"level":9},"one-run-across-shards","One run across shards",2,{"id":11,"text":12,"level":9},"share-the-report","Share the report",{"id":14,"text":15,"level":9},"trx-and-other-test-results","TRX and other test results","docs/ci.md"],"uses":{"params":["page","product"]}}]}
